because you have cops
that, when you do that,

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:39.615
they take it personal, and
they will be after you forever.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:43.202
When you think it's over,
there's still one going,

00:11:43.202 --> 00:11:45.996
"Oh, you wrote that letter, right? Wait."

00:11:45.996 --> 00:11:47.081
They don't like it.

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:48.541
Arthur: If you tease them,
Oky: Don't do that.

00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:49.792
they're going to take it personal.

00:11:49.792 --> 00:11:51.877
Normally a professional
won't take these diamonds.

00:11:51.877 --> 00:11:54.505
No, that's the thing. You
have to cut these diamonds.

00:11:54.505 --> 00:11:55.631
You know, they are known,

00:11:55.631 --> 00:11:58.175
so you have to find
someone who can cut them

00:11:58.175 --> 00:12:00.219
before you can sell
them on the black market

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:01.929
or on the legal market.

00:12:01.929 --> 00:12:04.181
But when you steal diamonds from a museum,

00:12:04.181 --> 00:12:05.433
old diamonds,

00:12:05.433 --> 00:12:09.186
especially when they are
in some kind of jewelry,

00:12:09.186 --> 00:12:11.981
they are not really important diamonds.

00:12:11.981 --> 00:12:14.191
So you can't hardly cut them.

00:12:14.191 --> 00:12:15.901
So when people steal old diamonds,

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:18.612
they probably didn't
do their homework well.

00:12:18.612 --> 00:12:20.614
[alarm ringing]

00:12:25.077 --> 00:12:28.330
It would be better if you
would have shown in this clip

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:31.041
Inspector Clouseau, the clumsy inspector

00:12:31.041 --> 00:12:32.960
who is going to have to solve this case,

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:34.795
because, you know, when I tracked down

00:12:34.795 --> 00:12:37.173
a piece of stolen art
of Picasso or whatever,

00:12:37.173 --> 00:12:39.717
they called me "the Indiana
Jones of art" in the media.

00:12:39.717 --> 00:12:43.637
But in reality, I'm more
like Clouseau, you know?

00:12:43.637 --> 00:12:47.391
Like this inspector, I
follow the wrong suspects.

00:12:47.391 --> 00:12:50.478
They feed me with false leads.

00:12:50.478 --> 00:12:51.771
When I disguise myself,

00:12:51.771 --> 00:12:53.981
I look more ridiculous
than Inspector Clouseau

00:12:53.981 --> 00:12:56.400
when he was disguised as a pirate

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.903
with an inflatable parrot on his shoulder.

00:12:58.903 --> 00:13:00.571
But we do have one thing in common,

00:13:00.571 --> 00:13:02.323
and that's that we never give up,

00:13:02.323 --> 00:13:03.949
and nobody takes him serious,

00:13:03.949 --> 00:13:05.701
but in the end he cracks the case.

00:13:05.701 --> 00:13:07.578
I would rate this movie a 10.

00:13:07.578 --> 00:13:10.915
4, 5. You know, I mean,

00:13:10.915 --> 00:13:12.500
it's too Hollywood.

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:18.047
There you go!

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:24.303
They got it right in this movie.

00:13:24.303 --> 00:13:26.096
Because normally in
these Hollywood movies,

00:13:26.096 --> 00:13:26.931
they go through the door,

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:28.349
but nobody goes through the door.

00:13:28.349 --> 00:13:29.517
It's the most protected path.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.775
This robbery has something to
do with a case that I solved.

00:13:37.775 --> 00:13:39.568
That was the ring of Oscar Wilde.

00:13:39.568 --> 00:13:43.364
It was stolen in 2002
in Oxford University.

00:13:43.364 --> 00:13:46.367
The thief got caught, but he told police

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:47.868
that he had melted the ring down.

00:13:47.868 --> 00:13:51.664
Nobody knew that he had
sold the ring to somebody.

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:54.959
And this guy had stored this same ring

00:13:54.959 --> 00:13:58.587
in one of these safety
deposits in Hatton Garden.

00:13:58.587 --> 00:14:00.214
What I'm doing is profiling.

00:14:00.214 --> 00:14:02.842
You know, I'm thinking,
"What would I have done?"

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:08.222
When they showed footage of the burglary,

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:12.643
one of the thieves had rainbow socks on.

00:14:12.643 --> 00:14:14.144
Rainbow socks.

00:14:14.144 --> 00:14:17.022
And I said, "You check that heist."

00:14:17.022 --> 00:14:21.694
Because if it was young guys,
they would destroy the ring

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:23.696
or they would sell it or throw it away.

00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:27.032
But only older men have the respect

00:14:27.032 --> 00:14:29.285
for the real value of this ring.

00:14:29.285 --> 00:14:31.704
So it got stolen again in 2015.

00:14:31.704 --> 00:14:33.873
Of course, this guy couldn't
go to the police and say,

00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:36.375
"Look, they have stolen
my Oscar Wilde ring,"

00:14:36.375 --> 00:14:37.835
because it was stolen before.

00:14:37.835 --> 00:14:39.295
I heard rumors on the market

00:14:39.295 --> 00:14:43.173
that a certain ring had
appeared on the market

00:14:43.173 --> 00:14:45.217
appearing to be Oscar Wilde's ring.

00:14:45.217 --> 00:14:47.136
So Oky came to my home,

00:14:47.136 --> 00:14:49.096
and he said he did this heist,

00:14:49.096 --> 00:14:50.472
and in many of these safes,

00:14:50.472 --> 00:14:53.100
he found stolen art, you
know, stolen jewelry.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.351
So he gave me the leads,

00:14:54.351 --> 00:14:57.438
and then I approached these
guys, these senior guys,

00:14:57.438 --> 00:15:00.983
and, finally, I tracked the ring back.

00:15:00.983 --> 00:15:02.818
They had sold it because they didn't know

00:15:02.818 --> 00:15:03.777
it was the ring of Oscar Wilde.

00:15:03.777 --> 00:15:06.113
They sold it to a jewelry shop,

00:15:06.113 --> 00:15:07.573
which sold it to some guy.

00:15:07.573 --> 00:15:10.743
So it shows that stolen jewels

00:15:10.743 --> 00:15:12.369
do appear on the black market.

00:15:12.369 --> 00:15:14.538
In this case, even on the legal market.

00:15:14.538 --> 00:15:18.167
And the recovery rates for
art theft are very small.

00:15:18.167 --> 00:15:20.920
I think that around

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.422
7% or 8% is being recovered.

00:15:23.422 --> 00:15:24.965
The rest disappears forever.

00:15:26.842 --> 00:15:28.928
Basil: I'm just checking
for wear on the pads.

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:31.639
You can work out the code by
identifying the most worn ones.

00:15:31.639 --> 00:15:33.682
I opened doors like that. It's true.

00:15:33.682 --> 00:15:35.684
But then you have to know the combination,

00:15:35.684 --> 00:15:37.853
because if you have four times four,

00:15:37.853 --> 00:15:41.065
I don't know how many
combinations you have. Hundreds.

00:15:45.277 --> 00:15:47.321
There are different
ways to open this door.

00:15:47.321 --> 00:15:48.697
You don't need a key.

00:15:48.697 --> 00:15:52.576
He has a piece of paper,
I guess. Uncut key.

00:15:52.576 --> 00:15:54.662
He pulls it in, and when he flips it,

00:15:54.662 --> 00:15:58.082
he will see the guts of
the mechanism inside.

00:15:58.082 --> 00:16:00.292
And then cuts it off.
And then it will turn.

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:01.752
Let's give it a 10.

00:16:01.752 --> 00:16:03.170
A 10, because it really happened.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:05.756
And it's really what they do. It's real.

00:16:09.218 --> 00:16:12.429
This is the famous, the nitrogen.

00:16:12.429 --> 00:16:13.472
Very dangerous.

00:16:13.472 --> 00:16:16.016
If it falls like that on your finger,

00:16:16.016 --> 00:16:17.643
you can break it off.

00:16:17.643 --> 00:16:21.105
I know a few very serious burglars.

00:16:21.105 --> 00:16:22.147
They never used this.

00:16:23.524 --> 00:16:25.442
Joey: Hey, Nick.

00:16:25.442 --> 00:16:27.569
Nick: Joey, talk to me.
Tell me what you got.

00:16:27.569 --> 00:16:28.487
It's not here.

00:16:29.989 --> 00:16:32.741
But the distraction thing
is an interesting one.

00:16:32.741 --> 00:16:36.662
Somebody is threatening
to kill themselves.

00:16:36.662 --> 00:16:39.748
So everybody's distracted.
And this does happen.

00:16:39.748 --> 00:16:43.669
The most famous gang of
art thieves in the world

00:16:43.669 --> 00:16:44.670
are the Pink Panthers.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.380
They are named after the Hollywood film.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.881
They come from Yugoslavia,

00:16:47.881 --> 00:16:51.385
and this gang has both
male and female members.

00:16:51.385 --> 00:16:53.262
And they do use these tricks.

00:16:53.262 --> 00:16:56.098
In 2004 in the Louvre, there
was an antiquities fair,

00:16:56.098 --> 00:16:59.518
and there was a diamond worth $60 million.

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:03.147
It was on display. Bright
lights, security guards.

00:17:03.147 --> 00:17:05.399
But then the wife of the
French minister arrived,

00:17:05.399 --> 00:17:08.152
so everybody looked for
a second the other way.

00:17:08.152 --> 00:17:10.821
And when they looked back,
the diamond was gone.

00:17:10.821 --> 00:17:13.824
In another case, they painted a bench

00:17:13.824 --> 00:17:16.452
in front of a jewelry shop in Miami

00:17:16.452 --> 00:17:17.786
that they were about to hit

00:17:17.786 --> 00:17:19.747
because they didn't want
anybody to sit there

00:17:19.747 --> 00:17:21.623
and become a witness on what they do.

00:17:21.623 --> 00:17:24.752
They used pretty women to distract guards

00:17:24.752 --> 00:17:27.421
and all stuff like that.
So this does happen.

00:17:27.421 --> 00:17:31.216
If you got a wrong mechanism inside,

00:17:31.216 --> 00:17:32.926
the whole door locks.

00:17:32.926 --> 00:17:34.094
You don't open it.

00:17:34.094 --> 00:17:35.929
You won't open it again, you know?

00:17:35.929 --> 00:17:38.015
Yeah. Also a 4 or a 3.

00:17:38.015 --> 00:17:40.893
I mean, you get caught
if you do it like that.

00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:42.019
Thank you for watching.

00:17:42.019 --> 00:17:44.772
If you want to see more,
click the video above.

